include includes/folio-shared.pug

html(lang="en")
    head
        +folioHead('A certificate can expire without changing the product | Aaron Benjamin', 'I designed Green Loom’s retail catalog so a hemp product’s lab certificate can expire, and stop a store listing, without changing the product itself.', '/green-loom.html', 'folio', '/assets/img/folio/project--green-loom/green-loom--catalog-hero-2x.png')
    body.folio.folio--case.folio--green-case
        +folioShell

        +folioHeader('work', 'Green Loom', '/#green-loom')

        main#main.folio-main.folio-main--case
            article.folio-case.green-case
                header.folio-case__hero
                    .folio-case__hero-media.green-case__hero-media
                        +figurePlaceholder('The Corner Store catalog. Peach Orchard Chew is open as an edible. Category is not Flower. Form is not Inhalable. No “Help-derived” text. Stock states are visible. The product and its certificate read as separate facts.', '960×660', '1920×1320', '/assets/img/folio/project--green-loom/green-loom--catalog-hero-2x.png', 'src/assets/img/folio/project--green-loom/green-loom--catalog-hero-2x.png', 'Catalog workspace for The Corner Store, with Peach Orchard Chew open as an edible and its certificate separate from the product.')
                    .folio-case__intro
                        p.folio-eyebrow Case study
                        h1 A certificate can expire without changing the product
                        p.folio-case__dek As Green Loom’s co-founder, I designed a retail catalog where a product’s lab certificate is its own record. When the certificate runs out, the store listing stops and the product stays as it was.
                    +caseFacts
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Role
                            dd Co-founder
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Scope
                            dd Retail catalog model and operator workflow
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Key decision
                            dd Keep lab evidence separate from product identity
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Outcome
                            dd Selected design direction, approved wireframe baseline, and a public schema

                +caseSection({labelledby: "green-rule-title"})
                    h2#green-rule-title Georgia requires a lab certificate from the last 12 months
                    p Green Loom’s retail launch scope is Georgia consumable hemp. Georgia prohibits retail sale of hemp flower and leaves. It requires a full-panel certificate of analysis, or COA, contracted within the last 12 months and made publicly available. Product labels must show the analysis results or give direct QR access to them.
                    p The rule puts a date on the evidence, not on the product. Nothing about a product has to change for its certificate to go out of date. As co-founder, I had to decide where that certificate lives in the catalog, and what happens to a product when its certificate runs out.
                    p.green-case__source-note Design context:&#32;
                        a(href="https://www.agr.georgia.gov/hemp-retail-consumable-hemp-licenses" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer") Georgia Department of Agriculture retail guidance
                        | &#32;and&#32;
                        a(href="https://rules.sos.ga.gov/gac/40-32-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer") Georgia’s consumable-hemp rules
                        | . These sources informed the design. Hemp rules change, so check the current rules rather than this page. This case study is not a claim of legal certification.

                +caseSection({labelledby: "green-evidence-title", tint: true, prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        h2#green-evidence-title I kept the certificate out of the product record
                        p Early catalog concepts put product details, store listings, stock, and lab results on the same screens. They looked like fields on one product, even though they follow different rules. Potency looked editable on the product, although the lab result should be its source.
                        p I gave the certificate its own record, a Lab Result, with its own validity period. When a certificate expires, the proposed publication check stops the store listing and names the reason. The product keeps its identity. Linking a new certificate lets the listing publish again without re-entering the product.
                    +stepList({labelledby: "green-timeline-caption", variant: "green-case__diagram--timeline", title: "When a certificate expires", steps: [{term: "Certificate linked", detail: "A Lab Result is linked to Peach Orchard Chew. The Corner Store listing can publish."}, {term: "Certificate expires", detail: "The Lab Result is past its validity period."}, {term: "Listing stopped", detail: "The publication check blocks The Corner Store listing and names the expired certificate."}, {term: "Product unchanged", detail: "Peach Orchard Chew keeps its name, description, and packages."}, {term: "New certificate", detail: "A new Lab Result is linked, and the listing can publish again."}], note: "The product record stays the same through every step. Only the evidence and the listing’s status change.", caption: "A designed flow, not a shipped screen. Peach Orchard Chew and The Corner Store are sample data, not a real product or shop.", captionId: "green-timeline-caption"})

                +caseSection({labelledby: "green-direction-title", prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        h2#green-direction-title One model, two speeds of work
                        p The same split runs through the rest of the catalog. Product holds shared identity and content. Variant identifies a sellable package. Listing controls how a variant is offered at a store. Inventory records quantity and availability. Lab Result holds the certificate. The interface can show these facts together without making them one record.
                    +objectPath({labelledby: "green-model-caption", kicker: "Green Loom catalog model", mark: "Sample data", nodes: [{label: "Product", detail: "Peach Orchard Chew"}, {label: "Variant", detail: "A sellable package of the chew"}, {label: "Listing", detail: "Price and publication at The Corner Store"}], support: [{term: "Lab Result", detail: "The chew’s certificate, with its own validity period"}, {term: "Inventory", detail: "Stock at The Corner Store"}], caption: "Product holds shared content, Variant is the package, and Listing is how that package is sold at one store. Lab Result and Inventory stay separate. Peach Orchard Chew and The Corner Store are sample data.", captionId: "green-model-caption"})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p The interface followed the model. A guided first-publish path helps a new operator create the needed records in order. Returning operators work directly in catalog and inventory views instead of reopening a wizard for every change.
                        p We chose this object-first approach after comparing it with a journey-led launchpad and a cross-object work queue. The tradeoff is that direct workspaces suit routine work, but the first-use path has to explain unfamiliar terms without showing every underlying record at once. The selected concept and initial wireframe baseline were approved as design direction, not as usability findings.
                    +caseFigure({class: "green-case__figure green-case__mobile-figure", labelledby: "green-mobile-caption", src: "/assets/img/folio/project--green-loom/green-loom--mobile-catalog-2x.png", alt: "Mobile catalog with Published and Draft groups, product rows, stock states, search, and an add-product control", width: "402", height: "874", loading: "lazy", caption: "Figma exploration of the mobile catalog. Published and draft products are grouped, with stock states on each row and search and add-product controls at the bottom. The rows repeat one sample product name.", captionId: "green-mobile-caption"})

                aside.folio-case__section.folio-case__section--tint.green-case__aside(aria-labelledby="green-schema-title")
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow For technical readers
                        h2#green-schema-title A schema for exchanged data, separate from the product model
                        p No existing standard or vendor format gave this catalog a complete product-data contract, so Green Loom published the versioned&#32;
                            a(href="https://github.com/greenloom/cannabis-product-schema" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer") Cannabis Product Schema
                            | . Its jurisdiction-neutral core describes Product, Package, LabResult, Label, and Party. A separate Georgia hemp profile adds local constraints without building them into the core.
                        p The schema makes incoming and outgoing product data checkable. Retail still needs its own Product, Variant, Listing, Inventory, and Lab Result records so an operator edits the right thing. Publishing the schema is a concrete output. It is not evidence of industry adoption or legal approval.
                    figure.folio-case__figure.green-case__figure(aria-labelledby="green-schema-caption")
                        .green-case__diagram.green-case__diagram--schema
                            .green-case__schema-core
                                p.green-case__diagram-title Jurisdiction-neutral core
                                ul
                                    li Product
                                    li Package
                                    li LabResult
                                    li Label
                                    li Party
                            .green-case__schema-profile
                                p.green-case__diagram-title Georgia hemp profile
                                p Allowed forms, testing evidence, labeling, and potency constraints
                                span.green-case__schema-rule Applied to core data, not embedded in core
                        figcaption#green-schema-caption Five document types form the neutral core. The Georgia profile applies state-specific constraints to those documents.

                +caseOutcome("green-outcome-title", "Approved as design direction, with operator evidence still ahead")
                    p The work produced a selected information architecture, an approved wireframe baseline, and a published schema. Later high-fidelity work is still a design candidate. An operator pilot is active. This page was written in September 2026 and does not record when the pilot started. Direct operator evidence, accessibility review, and other specialist checks are still needed before I can say the experience works in practice.
                    p My takeaway is that simplifying a regulated workflow does not mean hiding its rules. It means keeping the right evidence authoritative and showing people why an action is available, blocked, or incomplete.

            +folioFooter(true, 'I’m glad to walk through the Green Loom model, the schema, or where the pilot stands.', {href: '/design-dash.html', label: 'Next case: Design Dash'})
